Why Do We Need Arguments?

Consider two workflows:

Main.xaml – Receives a number from the user.
AnalyzeNumber.xaml – Checks whether the number is Odd/Even, Positive/Negative, etc.

The question is:

How does AnalyzeNumber.xaml get the number from Main.xaml?
How does Main.xaml receive the result from AnalyzeNumber.xaml?

✅ Arguments act as a communication bridge between workflows. 
They are used to send and receive data between different XAML files.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Properties of Arguments
1. Name

The Name property specifies the unique name of the argument.

Example:

Plain Text
in_Number
out_Result
-------------------------------------------------------
2. Direction

The Direction property specifies how data flows.

Direction	Purpose
In	Receives data into the workflow
Out	Sends data out of the workflow
In/Out	Receives and returns data
Property	Used in library components

Example:

Plain Text
in_Number → Input Argument
out_Message → Output Argument
-------------------------------------------------
3. Argument Type

The Argument Type property specifies the type of data stored in the argument.

Examples:

String
Int32
Boolean
Object
DataTable
Array
DateTime
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Default Value

The Default Value property specifies the initial value assigned to the argument.

Example:

Plain Text
in_Name = "Harini"
in_Number = 10

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. IsRequired

The IsRequired property makes an argument mandatory.

✅ If selected, a value must be provided when the workflow is called.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Additional Argument Options

(i) Convert to Variable

Converts an argument into a variable of the same data type.

(ii)Copy

Copies arguments along with their name, type, and default value to another workflow.

(iii)Paste

Pastes copied arguments into another workflow.

(iv)Delete

Removes the argument from the Arguments panel.

(v)Design Properties

Allows you to add or edit comments and tooltips for the argument.

(vi)Add Watch

Adds the argument to the Watch panel during debugging.

(vii)Find References

Displays all places where the argument is used in the project.
